==Icelandic==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|non|is}} {{term|dauðr|lang=non}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|is}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*daudaz}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈtøyːðʏr/|lang=is}}
*: {{rhymes|øyːðʏr|lang=is}}

===Adjective===
{{is-adj-01|dauð|dautt|dauðari|dauðastur}}

# [[dead]]

====Inflection====
{{is-decl-adj-1|d|au|ð}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|dead}} {{l|is|dáinn}}, {{l|is|látinn}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|is|dauður sjór}} ([[sea]] where no fish is caught)
* {{l|is|fyrr skal ég dauður liggja}} (over my dead body)
* {{l|is|nýdauður}}
* {{l|is|steindauður}}
* {{l|is|víndauður}} (passed out from drinking)

====Related terms====
* {{l|is|dauði}}

====Usage notes====
* '''''dauður''''' can sound heartless in modern Icelandic when used for someone the speaker, or the people he is speaking to, knew. When referring to a specific person, use {{term|dáinn|lang=is}} or {{term|látinn|lang=is}} instead, unless you are talking about a historical figure or someone otherwise unconnected to you.

